The G9 structural arm has exposed the current weakness: one vendor solved after being told the parity invariant, so G9(b) fails at this rung. The rules permit one harder rerun. I’m using the remaining fixed-length axis: show several equally plausible affine base keys, exactly one of which expands to a list-valid coloring. After recognizing parity, wrong keys can be eliminated by cheap list-membership probes and the final coloring still needs only 114 XORs; without that selection step, the hint no longer hands over the whole route.
